From 6aff9663cbe7b4105387c046fa3cd18225ec5a8b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 19:25:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/6] Fix requests failing with "Invalid value" from a key with
 hidden characters
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

An API key pasted from a styled page or document can carry invisible
characters — zero-width space, non-breaking space, soft hyphen, a stray
newline. None of those are legal in an HTTP header value, so fetch threw
TypeError "Failed to execute 'fetch' on 'Window': Invalid value" locally,
before any request went out, on every transport. getOpenAiKey only
trimmed, which catches a trailing newline but nothing interior.

The XHR transport made it much harder to read: setRequestHeader was
wrapped in an empty catch, so instead of failing it sent the request with
no Authorization header at all. That came back 401 "You didn't provide an
API key", which looks exactly like a rejected key, and sent you off
rotating a key that was never the problem.

- sanitizeKey/readStoredKey scrub stored keys to printable ASCII, on save
  and on read, and rewrite the cleaned value back
- assertHeaderSafe fails with a sentence that names the cause
- XHR transport now fails loudly instead of sending an unauthenticated
  request
- explainError distinguishes "key never arrived" from "key rejected"

Also stop sending the key as ?key= in the URL. Query strings land in
browser history, Referer headers, proxy logs and screenshots; the key now
travels in the request body instead, which the worker strips before
forwarding. The worker no longer forwards "Authorization: null" when no
key reaches it, and returns a clear error instead.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_019GqTQm8E1znRkM37YVj6K7
